#/target

Cargo.lock
*.odg#
*.ods#


# Upstream reference clones (if ever added here) -- never committed.
/upstream_source/*/

# V&V raw data. Every .md write-up in verification_and_validation/ is committed
# -- both the hand-written ones and the machine-generated reports under
# generated/ -- because the narrative and the numbers are the durable record and
# stay small. Standalone .csv data files are NOT committed, at any depth:
# regenerate them by re-running the test or example that produced them.
/verification_and_validation/*.csv
/verification_and_validation/**/*.csv

# EXCEPTION -- golden references ARE committed. A golden reference is not
# regenerable raw data: it is a trajectory recorded under a build configuration
# that is no longer the default, kept so the current code can be diffed against
# it. `golden/<case>/` therefore holds data that must survive, and each such
# directory is documented by a sibling .md giving the commit, the machine, the
# exact command and the acceptance criterion. Keep these small and few.
!/verification_and_validation/golden/**/*.csv

# Generated output of the `steam_table_plotter` example (GitHub issue #26).
# Regenerate the whole directory in a few seconds with:
#
#   cargo run --release -p tampines-steam-tables --example steam_table_plotter -- --export-all
#
# It is ignored by default because the four PNG/PDF/SVG figure sets plus their
# curve and reference-point CSVs run to tens of megabytes, and every byte of it
# is reproducible from the command above. If a particular figure is wanted as a
# committed publication artifact, add that one file with `git add -f`.
/figures/
